<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#presidential-democracy">Glossary</a> · Term · government</p><h1 class="enc-title">Presidential democracy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1789">1789 CE</a> – present</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Presidential democracy is government by a president elected separately from the legislature for a fixed term, under a constitution that separates and balances powers.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The form differs from <b>presidential democracy</b>, in which a separately elected president serves a fixed term, and from semi-presidential systems such as the French Fifth Republic, in which an elected president shares power with a prime minister answerable to parliament.</blockquote><a href="/government/parliamentary-democracy">Parliamentary democracy · How It Works</a></li><li><blockquote>The new constitution kept much of the <b>presidential system</b> but made the office hereditary, and the dependence on Italian money continued.</blockquote><a href="/state/albanian-republic">Albanian Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>Nazarbayev ruled as president for nearly three decades, building a strong <b>presidential system</b> with little political competition.</blockquote><a href="/state/kazakhstan">Kazakhstan</a></li><li><blockquote>A <b>presidential system</b> modelled on the American constitution faced the communist-led Hukbalahap rising, defeated under Ramon Magsaysay in the 1950s.</blockquote><a href="/state/philippines">Philippines</a></li><li><blockquote>The Constitution of 1976 established a semi-<b>presidential democracy</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/third-portuguese-republic">Third Portuguese Republic</a></li></ul></section>
